              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Attila the Hun</strong><br>\nAttila, born around 406 AD, was the ruler of the Huns from 434 until his death in 453 AD. He is known for transforming the Huns into one of the most formidable forces in Europe. He was co-ruler with his brother, Bleda, until he assumed sole leadership. Little is known about Attila's early life, but it is believed that he grew up in a nomadic warrior culture, learning the arts of war and leadership from a young age.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Power</strong><br>\nAttila came to power during a time of turmoil and fragmentation in the Roman Empire. He capitalized on this weakness to assert Hunnic strength. By 432 AD, Attila and Bleda had secured a treaty with the Eastern Roman Empire that significantly increased Hunnic annual tributes. Attila’s aggressive diplomacy and strategic marriages helped consolidate his power and expand Hunnic territories.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Attila's Rule and Expansion</strong><br>\nUnder Attila's rule, the Huns' territory stretched from the Ural River to the Rhine River and from the Danube River to the Baltic Sea. Attila was a cunning ruler, known for his ability to play off the Roman Empire's Eastern and Western divisions. He used a combination of military pressure and shrewd negotiations to expand his influence and extract wealth from the Romans.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
