      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Huns' Military Tactics</strong><br>\nThe Huns, under Attila, were renowned for their innovative military tactics. They were master horsemen and archers, using speed and precision to overwhelm more heavily armored opponents. Their light cavalry could execute fast raids and retreat before the enemy could mount an effective counterattack, striking fear into the hearts of their adversaries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Diplomacy and Tribute</strong><br>\nAttila's understanding of power wasn't limited to warfare. He used threats as a diplomatic tool, demanding vast sums of gold from the Romans in exchange for peace. His demands were often met, as Roman leaders preferred paying tributes to facing the Huns in battle. This strategy filled the Hunnic coffers and strengthened Attila’s hand without prolonged warfare.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Invasion of Gaul</strong><br>\nIn 451 AD, Attila invaded Gaul, which was a territory under Roman influence. This campaign marked the zenith of Hunnic power and brought Attila into direct conflict with Roman general Flavius Aetius. Despite initial successes, Attila was checked at the Battle of the Catalaunian Plains, where Roman and Visigothic forces halted his advance. It was one of the few times Attila faced significant opposition.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
