            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Sack of Roman Cities</strong><br>\nAttila’s forces sacked numerous cities and exerted pressure on the Roman Empire's infrastructure. These sackings were brutal and exhaustive, leading to a widespread breakdown of Roman order. Attila's campaigns hastened the relocation of the Roman capital from Rome to Ravenna, which was more defensible against Hunnic attacks.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Retreat and Death</strong><br>\nDespite his reputation as the \"Scourge of God,\" Attila's campaign in Gaul was a high-water mark followed by retreat. His attempt to invade Italy in 452 was also stymied, partly due to disease and famine afflicting his army. Attila died in 453 AD under mysterious circumstances on his wedding night, leaving a power vacuum that would eventually lead to the Hunnic Empire's fragmentation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Attila's Legacy</strong><br>\nAttila's death led to the quick disintegration of the Hunnic Empire. His sons could not maintain the cohesion and power that Attila had wielded. Nevertheless, Attila's legacy as a fearsome leader who challenged the might of Rome lived on. He is remembered both as a ruthless conqueror and a shaper of the European map during the 5th century.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
